# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Audio Metadati Manager — Docker Compose environment
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy this file to ".env" and adjust the values for your machine:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# ".env" is git-ignored and stays on your computer only.
# This ".env.example" file is committed and contains no secrets.

# MUSIC_PATH — absolute path, on the HOST, of the folder containing your music.
# It is mounted inside the container as /music, which is the library root the
# application reads (AUDIO_LIBRARY_OVERRIDE=/music).
# Replace the placeholder below with your own folder, for example:
#   macOS / Linux : MUSIC_PATH=/Users/alice/Music
#   Windows       : MUSIC_PATH=C:/Users/Alice/Music
#   Linux         : MUSIC_PATH=/srv/music
# If this variable is not set, Compose falls back to "./music" (the folder in
# the project root), so it also works without creating a .env file.
#
# Notes:
#   - the folder must already exist, otherwise Docker creates an empty one;
#   - on macOS and Windows the folder must be shared with Docker Desktop;
#   - the container needs write access to it: it stores the metadata and the
#     embedded cover art in your audio files.
MUSIC_PATH=/absolute/path/to/your/music/folder

# ------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL — additional music folders (other disks)
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# MUSIC_PATH_2 and MUSIC_PATH_3 let you register music that lives on a
# different disk or on another machine. Inside the container each one gets its
# own stable path, so the application never confuses them:
#
#   MUSIC_PATH  -> /music
#   MUSIC_PATH_2 -> /music-roots/music2
#   MUSIC_PATH_3 -> /music-roots/music3
#
# They are entirely optional: leave them commented out (or empty) and the
# application behaves exactly as before, with a single root. When a variable is
# empty, Compose mounts the empty placeholder folder "docker-placeholder/musicN",
# which simply shows up in the interface as an empty root.
#
# Example with two music folders on two different disks:
#
#   MUSIC_PATH=/mnt/primary-disk/music
#   MUSIC_PATH_2=/mnt/secondary-disk/albums
#
# Then run:  docker compose up -d --build
#
# and open the "Cartelle musicali" button in the toolbar to choose which
# sub-folders of each root belong to your library.
#
# Notes:
#   - the host folder MUST exist: these mounts are declared with
#     create_host_path: false, so a wrong path makes Docker fail loudly instead
#     of silently creating an empty folder;
#   - adding a new host path to .env requires recreating the container
#     (docker compose up -d), because the mounts are fixed at start-up;
#   - the extra mounts are read-write, like /music, because the application
#     writes the tags and the embedded cover INTO the audio files. If you only
#     want to browse those roots without editing them, add "read_only: true" to
#     the matching entry in docker-compose.yml.
# MUSIC_PATH_2=/absolute/path/to/your/second/music/folder
# MUSIC_PATH_3=/absolute/path/to/your/third/music/folder
